\section{Several responses}{

The kriging weights depend only on the locations, the variogram model,
the trend and the folds, not on the observed values. A matrix response
with T columns is therefore cross-validated from one factorization: each
additional response costs a few matrix products (no further
factorization), and in buffered leave-one-out the hold-out sets and their
factorizations are shared too. Typical uses are many simulated fields,
several variables or time slices observed at the same locations, or
bootstrap replicates. \code{\link[=kcv_run]{kcv_run()}} with \code{y} cross-validates new responses
from an existing prepared object.

A response with one column (such as \code{scale(z) ~ 1}, or a one-column
matrix column) is a single response, as in \code{\link[stats:model.response]{stats::model.response()}},
unless the left-hand side is an explicit \code{cbind()}.

Every response is predicted at every location, so a missing or
non-finite value in any column is an error. Response names come from
the column names, else from the arguments of \code{cbind()}, else they are
\code{"1"}, \code{"2"}, .... Each response's rows are the output of the
single-response call, up to rounding in the last bits (with several
columns the BLAS uses matrix-matrix products). \code{\link[=kcv_metrics]{kcv_metrics()}}
summarizes each response separately. The output holds about
\eqn{10 n} numbers per response; several responses are checked against
the \code{fastkcv.max_gb} budget (see \code{\link[=kcv_engine]{kcv_engine()}}) left after the
factorization, and the error message gives a batch size that fits.
}

\section{Buffered leave-one-out}{

With \code{buffer = r}, observation \eqn{i} is held out together with every
observation within Euclidean distance \eqn{r} of it (distance
\eqn{\le r}, computed on the coordinates as given, without any variogram
anisotropy), and only \eqn{i} is predicted and reported. This removes the
spatially correlated neighbors that make ordinary leave-one-out
optimistic. \code{buffer = 0} gives ordinary leave-one-out exactly.

Hold-out sets of neighboring points overlap, so each point is a separate
kriging problem, solved from the same factorization with a hold-out set
\eqn{I} of size \eqn{m}, which needs the block \eqn{Q_{II}} of the
matrix \eqn{Q} described in \code{\link[=kcv_engine]{kcv_engine()}}. Two strategies are used,
whichever needs fewer operations:
\itemize{
\item per point: form \eqn{Q_{II}} directly, about \eqn{m^2 n} operations
per point and \eqn{8 m n} bytes of temporary memory (160 MB for
\eqn{m = 1000}, \eqn{n = 20000});
\item shared pairs: two points in a common hold-out set are at most
\eqn{2r} apart, so the entries of \eqn{Q} for all pairs of points
within \eqn{2r} are computed once (about \eqn{n} operations each,
roughly \eqn{2 \bar m n^2} in total) and each \eqn{Q_{II}} is
assembled from them. The cache takes 12 bytes per pair (about
\eqn{24 \bar m n} bytes) and is used only if it fits, together with the
factorization, in the \code{fastkcv.max_gb} budget (see \code{\link[=kcv_engine]{kcv_engine()}}).
}

Each point also needs a Cholesky factorization of \eqn{Q_{II}}
(\eqn{m^3 / 3} operations). Since \eqn{m} grows with the area of the
buffer, large radii are expensive at large \eqn{n}; \code{targets} restricts
the predictions to a sample of points, at a proportional saving. These
computations consist of many small matrix operations, which a
multithreaded BLAS does not speed up and can slow down: with OpenBLAS,
setting the environment variable \code{OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1} before starting
R is usually as fast or faster. Use \code{\link[=kcv_prepare]{kcv_prepare()}} and \code{\link[=kcv_run]{kcv_run()}} to try
several radii from one factorization.

An error names the first point that cannot be predicted: a buffer that
holds out every observation, or (universal kriging) one that removes all
observations that identify the trend, for example every observation of
one level of a factor.
}

\section{Performance}{

Almost all of the time goes into one dense Cholesky factorization and
triangular inverse, done by R's BLAS and LAPACK libraries, so an optimized
BLAS (OpenBLAS, Intel MKL, Apple's Accelerate) makes \code{kcv()} many times
faster than the reference BLAS. Timings, how to switch the BLAS, thread
settings, the cost of few large folds, and comparisons with other
packages are in \code{vignette("fastkcv")}; memory requirements are in
\code{\link[=kcv_engine]{kcv_engine()}}. To cross-validate several fold schemes from one
factorization, use \code{\link[=kcv_prepare]{kcv_prepare()}} and \code{\link[=kcv_run]{kcv_run()}}.
}

\section{Ill-conditioned covariance matrices}{

The factorization also estimates the reciprocal condition number of the
covariance matrix (LAPACK routine \code{dpocon}, in the 1-norm), shown by printing a
\code{\link[=kcv_prepare]{kcv_prepare()}} object. Predictions and variances have a relative error
of up to about \eqn{\epsilon / \mathrm{rcond}}, with \eqn{\epsilon
\approx 2.2 \times 10^{-16}} the machine precision, and \code{krige.cv()}
degrades in the same way. Below \code{getOption("fastkcv.rcond_warn", 1e-13)}
a warning is given: against a quadruple-precision computation, the
largest error in \code{var1.pred} was at most \eqn{1.2 \times 10^{-5}} of
the leave-one-out RMSE above that threshold and reached
\eqn{10^{-3}} just below it. Such matrices typically come from a
Gaussian (or another very smooth) model without a nugget, or from nearly
coincident locations; a small nugget usually restores the accuracy.
}

\section{Name clash with spmodel}{

spmodel also exports a function \code{kcv()}, for its fitted models. When
both packages are attached, the one attached last masks the other; call
\code{fastkcv::kcv()} explicitly. Given a fitted spmodel model, this \code{kcv()}
stops with a pointer to \code{spmodel::kcv()}.
}

\examples{
library(sp)
data(meuse, package = "sp")
v <- gstat::vgm(0.59, "Sph", 897, 0.05)
cv <- kcv(log(zinc) ~ 1, ~ x + y, meuse, model = v)
head(cv)
sqrt(mean(cv$residual^2))

# Buffered leave-one-out: neighbors within 300 m are held out too.
cv_buf <- kcv(log(zinc) ~ 1, ~ x + y, meuse, model = v, buffer = 300)
summary(cv_buf$n_heldout)
kcv_metrics(cv_buf)

# The same statistics from a sample of 50 points.
set.seed(1)
kcv_metrics(kcv(log(zinc) ~ 1, ~ x + y, meuse, model = v, buffer = 300,
  targets = sample(nrow(meuse), 50)))

# Several responses with the same model, trend and folds.
cv3 <- kcv(cbind(log(zinc), log(copper), log(lead)) ~ 1, ~ x + y, meuse,
  model = v, nfold = 5)
kcv_metrics(cv3)
}
